---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import GetSalvage from "../../components/GetSalvage.astro";
import doctor from "../../examples/doctor.txt?raw";
import doctorJson from "../../examples/doctor.json.txt?raw";

// The twelve checks, in the order doctor runs them.
const groups = [
  {
    title: "Engine",
    checks: [
      {
        name: "Engine install",
        what: "Finds an Unreal Engine install and reports its version and whether it's a Launcher build or a source build. If it can't find one, it warns, and the checks that need an engine are skipped.",
      },
      {
        name: "Mac shader formats",
        what: "Reads the Metal shader formats targeted for Mac, from the engine's config and, with --project, your project's. One format passes. Two or more is a warning: each one is compiled and cached separately when you cook, so two formats is roughly double the shader work and cache. None fails, because nothing can render.",
        mac: true,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Xcode and Metal",
    checks: [
      {
        name: "Xcode",
        what: "Runs xcodebuild -version and compares the result with the range of Xcode versions the engine declares in its own config, and with Epic's documented versions for that engine. Fails if Xcode is missing, if only the Command Line Tools are active, if its license hasn't been accepted, or if the version is outside the engine's range. Warns if the engine accepts it but Epic lists it as not compatible or below its minimum. Notes the version Epic recommends and builds with, and points to salvage xcode.",
        mac: true,
      },
      {
        name: "xcode-select",
        what: "Checks that the developer tools point at a full Xcode, not the Command Line Tools or a folder that no longer exists. Notes when DEVELOPER_DIR overrides it.",
        mac: true,
      },
      {
        name: "Metal Toolchain",
        what: "Runs xcrun metal --version. Since Xcode 26 the Metal compiler is a separate download of about 1 GB, and without it the engine can't compile shaders. Fails if it's missing, and gives the download command.",
        mac: true,
      },
      {
        name: "Rosetta",
        what: "On Apple silicon, warns if Rosetta isn't installed: some engine tools and third-party plugins still ship Intel-only binaries. Skipped on Intel Macs.",
        mac: true,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Disk",
    checks: [
      {
        name: "Free space",
        what: "Free space on each volume that holds the engine, the project and your home folder. Fails under 10 GB and warns under 30 GB.",
      },
      {
        name: "Spaces in paths",
        what: "Warns when the project or your home folder has a space in its path, which trips up shell build steps and some plugins. A space in the engine path passes, since Epic's tools cope and the Launcher's default folder has one.",
      },
      {
        name: "Zen / DDC",
        what: "Adds up the local caches: Zen storage and the shared derived data cache in your user folder, the engine's and project's DerivedDataCache folders, and any UE-LocalDataCachePath or UE-ZenSubprocessDataPath override. Warns above 64 GB and names the biggest one.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Memory",
    checks: [
      {
        name: "RAM",
        what: "Warns under 16 GB, which is tight for the editor. Passes from 16 GB, and notes when you're under the 32 GB Epic recommends.",
      },
      {
        name: "Swap",
        what: "Warns when the swap in use is more than half your RAM, which means the system is paging to disk.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Debugging",
    checks: [
      {
        name: "~/.lldbinit",
        what: "Checks that ~/.lldbinit (or ~/.lldbinit-Xcode) loads formatters for Unreal types, Salvage's or the engine's own, so FString, TSharedPtr, TObjectPtr and friends show up as values in the debugger instead of raw memory. Warns if none are loaded or the line points at a file that's gone, and suggests salvage lldb install.",
        mac: true,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Page
  title="Check a Mac for Unreal"
  compact
  description="How to run salvage doctor, what its 12 checks look at, and how to read what it prints."
  dek="salvage doctor runs 12 checks for the things that make Unreal Engine slow or broken on a Mac, and says how to fix each one. It reads your setup and changes nothing."
>
  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <p>
      From any folder, run <code>salvage doctor</code>. Run it from a project's folder, or
      pass <code>--project</code>, and the shader check looks at that project's settings as well as the engine's.
    </p>
    <pre><code>salvage doctor
salvage doctor --project ~/Projects/MyGame
salvage doctor --engine "/Users/Shared/Epic Games/UE_5.8"
salvage doctor --json</code></pre>
    <p>
      Doctor finds the engine on its own. It takes the first of these that exists: the path in <code>--engine</code> or the <code>SALVAGE_ENGINE</code> environment variable, the engine your project is associated with, the newest
      engine the Epic Games Launcher has installed, and the newest engine in the Launcher's default folder.
    </p>

    <h2>What it prints</h2>
    <p>Here is doctor on a 16 GB Apple M4 Mac with Unreal Engine 5.8 installed from the Launcher:</p>
    <Terminal
      command="salvage doctor"
      output={doctor}
      caption="Real output from one machine, November 2026. Nine checks pass and three are worth a look."
    />
    <p>Each line starts with a mark:</p>
    <dl class="marks">
      <div><dt class="ok">✓</dt><dd>Passes. Nothing to do.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="warn">!</dt><dd>Worth a look. It works, but it's slower or harder than it needs to be.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="bad">✗</dt><dd>Needs fixing. Unreal won't build, render or debug properly until it is.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="dim">–</dt><dd>Doesn't apply here, such as Rosetta on an Intel Mac.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Anything that isn't a pass comes with an arrow and what to do about it, and often the exact command. Doctor
      never runs those commands for you. Some need <code>sudo</code>, so read them before you paste them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Doctor exits with 0 when nothing fails, even if some checks are worth a look, and 1 when anything fails. It exits
      with 2 if it can't start, for example when <code>--engine</code> points at a folder that isn't an engine.
    </p>

    <h2>The 12 checks</h2>
    {groups.map((group) => (
      <>
        <h3>{group.title}</h3>
        <dl class="checks">
          {group.checks.map((c) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{c.name}</dt>
              <dd>{c.what}{c.mac && <span class="mac"> macOS only.</span>}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </>
    ))}

    <h2>Which versions Epic supports</h2>
    <p>
      Epic's <a href="https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/macos-development-requirements-for-unreal-engine">macOS
      Development Requirements</a> page is the authority on which macOS and Xcode versions each engine release
      supports. Doctor checks something more mechanical: the Xcode range the engine's own config accepts, which is
      what the engine's SDK check enforces when you build.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two can differ. For 5.8, Epic's page lists Xcode 26.0 as the minimum and 26.1.1 as recommended, and says
      Xcode 26.4 isn't compatible. The engine's config accepts 15.2 to 27.9. Salvage keeps what the page says as a small
      table, with the date it was checked, so doctor fails a version the engine rejects and warns about one the engine
      accepts but Epic warns against.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/charts/salvage-xcode/"><code>salvage xcode</code></a> goes further: it judges every installed Xcode the same way, checks each one's Metal
      tools, and prints the <code>DEVELOPER_DIR</code> to pin the right one for that engine, per build, without <code>sudo xcode-select</code>. For installing another Xcode it hands off to <a href="https://github.com/XcodesOrg/xcodes">xcodes</a>, printing the <code>xcodes install</code> command.
    </p>

    <h2>On Windows and Linux</h2>
    <p>
      Doctor runs there too, with six checks: the engine install, free space, spaces in paths, Zen and DDC, RAM and
      swap. The other six only apply on macOS, and doctor leaves them out of the list and says so.
    </p>

    <h2>JSON</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--json</code> prints the same report for scripts. <code>schema</code> is the version of the layout, and
      goes up if it changes in a way that breaks readers. Each check has an <code>id</code>, a <code>status</code> (<code>pass</code>, <code>warn</code>, <code>fail</code> or <code>skip</code>), a <code>summary</code>, and <code>details</code> and a <code>fix</code> when there are any. Memory is in bytes.
    </p>
    <Terminal
      command="salvage doctor --json"
      output={doctorJson}
      marks={false}
      caption="The same run as above, cut down to two of the twelve checks."
    />

    <GetSalvage />
  </div>
</Page>
<style>
  .marks {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-2);
  }
  .marks > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .marks dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }
  .marks dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .ok {
    color: var(--link);
  }
  .warn {
    color: var(--mark);
  }
  .bad {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .dim {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .checks {
    margin: var(--s-3) 0 0;
  }
  .checks > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 11rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    padding-block: var(--s-4);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .checks dt {
    font-stretch: var(--stretch-sign);
    font-weight: 750;
  }
  .checks dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .mac {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .checks > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-1);
    }
  }
</style>
